<!-- cell: title-page id="8b2c4bae-1473-451b-a0ea-87029d6477b2" title="Dry Fork" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="7ac52512-d3c9-4d09-9981-0ab11aa3e88c" -->

The creek at Dry Fork stopped running on the ninth of July. By the twelfth, Hattie Crane had lost four steers to the heat and had stopped sleeping.

She rode up the valley at dawn with her son Eli and found the reason two miles above her land: a wall of rock and timber across the creek bed, and behind it a long brown lake glittering on Silas Boone's side of the fence, guarded by two of his riders with rifles across their knees.

"He's done it to the Mendozas too," said Eli. "And old Pryor. He wants the whole valley, Ma. He'll dry us out and buy us cheap." "I know what he wants," said Hattie.

That night Eli cleaned his father's rifle at the kitchen table. Hattie watched him for a while, then took it from him and hung it back over the door. "Your father died on the end of one of those," she said. "I'm not burying you on the end of another."

Instead she took down the tin box where Ben had kept the deed and the survey he had paid for in '81, when the territory was still arguing about where anything began. She copied it out in her neatest hand, and in the morning she sent Eli south to Tucson with the copy, to the territorial marshal. "Ride hard," she told him, "and don't stop for anybody wearing Boone's brand."

Two days later she rode alone to the Boone ranch.

Silas Boone received her on his porch, a broad, pleasant man who smiled with everything except his eyes. "Mrs. Crane. Sorry to hear about your stock. My offer for your land still stands."

She unfolded the survey on his porch rail and held it flat against the wind. "The upper bend of the creek, where your dam sits, is on my land. Ben paid a surveyor two hundred dollars to say so. You built your wall on Crane property, Mr. Boone, and you've been stealing my water with it."

Boone stopped smiling. "Paper burns, Mrs. Crane." "It does," said Hattie. "That's why there's a copy in Tucson with the territorial marshal, and my boy is bringing him here."

For a long moment the only sound was the wind in the cottonwoods. Then Boone turned to his foreman. "Open the dam."

The creek came down the valley that evening, brown and fast and loud, past the Mendozas and old Pryor and into the dry bed below the Crane house. Hattie stood in it up to her ankles and let it run over her boots, and for the first time since July she laughed.

Eli came home a week later with the marshal and a sunburn, and a story about his ride that he would tell for the rest of his life.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="8c4cb814-3490-4d03-9161-cf3fc2fff361" title="Hattie Crane" words="12-27, 28-47, 110-116, 128-166, 167-236, 237-246, 280-335, 343-364, 415-438" -->

A widowed rancher, stubborn and unafraid, who lost her husband to a rifle and will not lose her son the same way. She fights for her land with paper and nerve instead of guns.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="ac3f988f-55ce-4705-b5b0-64f56ae99286" title="Silas Boone" words="48-83, 84-109, 247-279, 336-342, 378-386" -->

A cattle baron, pleasant on the surface and ruthless beneath, who wants the whole valley and will dry out his neighbours to buy them cheap.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="863c419e-0115-41d6-b888-a5c556aaf30a" title="Dry Fork" words="0-11, 28-83, 167-198, 295-335, 365-377, 387-414" -->

The valley in the Arizona Territory and the creek it lives by, running past Boone's land, the Mendozas, old Pryor and the Crane house.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="12431fac-b19c-42c0-b709-f005e52ef7a2" title="Greed" words="48-83, 84-116, 247-279, 295-335, 336-342, 378-386" -->

Boone's hunger for the whole valley, pursued by damming its water.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="4f6e0248-fce6-4530-9c59-6fc6b47e7ed4" title="Courage" words="117-145, 199-236, 237-246, 280-335, 343-364, 439-466" -->

Hattie riding alone to Boone's ranch and calling his bluff, and her son's hard ride to Tucson for the marshal.

<!-- cell: plot id="1a9331e1-aef7-48dc-b67b-52f02382c239" title="The dam" words="0-27, 28-83, 167-198, 237-246, 280-335, 336-364, 378-386, 387-414" -->

Boone dams the creek and Hattie's cattle die; she finds her late husband's survey proving the dam is on her land, confronts Boone with it and with a copy held by the marshal, and he opens the dam.

<!-- cell: plot id="5e84e108-c3c7-4cbe-a9b8-3216b76799d4" title="Eli and the rifle" words="117-166, 199-236, 439-466" -->

Hattie's son wants to fight with his father's rifle; she takes it from him and sends him to Tucson with a copy of the survey instead, and he comes home with the marshal.
